{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>When you go out to war against your enemies, etc.</b> One needs to know why all this was necessary — should it not have said only ״when you see among the captives a woman״ etc.? Further, after it said ״when you go out to war,״ there was no need to say ״against your enemies.״ Perhaps it is because since the verse comes to permit the beautiful woman who is forbidden outside of wartime, and also to permit קדלי דחזירי (Ḥullin 17a) and other forbidden things, the verse was concerned that upon Israel's going out to war their feet would run to evil in order to benefit from something forbidden. Therefore the verse came and stipulated, saying <b>when you go out</b> — your going out to war shall be for the purpose of taking vengeance upon your enemies, who are the enemies of HaShem, in the manner of what it says (Psalms 139:21) ״Do I not hate those who hate You, O HaShem … they have become my enemies.״ For this purpose shall your going out be, and if you do so — ״HaShem your Elohim will deliver him into your hand and you shall take his captives.״ And from this you learn that otherwise, if your going out is for a different purpose — the reason being understood from what it says in the matter — it is not assured that HaShem will deliver him into his hand." "<b>And you shall take his captives.</b> It is a commandment, so that it not appear as trifling with the king's gift. Or it may intend to forbid the beautiful woman as long as you have not yet taken captives, so that a person not say: since she is permitted to me, I shall seize her and take her and lie with her — Scripture therefore says <b>and you shall take his captives and you shall see among the captives</b>, meaning: when you take her and she becomes yours, if you see among the captives etc. And the reason it said <b>his captives</b> and did not say ״him״ — our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sifrei) that the verse speaks of an optional war, and it was precise to say <b>his captives</b> even if he is from the Canaanite nations, about whom it is said (Deuteronomy 20:16) ״you shall not leave alive any soul״." "<b>Further,</b> it intends: since it is said ״HaShem will deliver him etc. into your hand,״ therefore when it says <b>and you shall take</b>, it finishes by saying <b>his captives</b> — meaning, that which is already captured and standing before you. It further alludes that even though you may be in the category of his captive relative to the measure applied to the warrior who is engaged in battle — even so, <b>you shall take</b>. And from this your eyes will behold that HaShem is the One who gives him over before you, and not your strength and the might of your hand that prevails."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:18:24.388317506Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}